editorials.passage:
&lt;p class=&quot;p1&quot;&gt;Every Sunday morning for the past twelve years, a stretch of road in Gurugram that carries cars the rest of the week has been handed back to people walking and cycling. What I find most instructive about Raahgiri Day is not the crowd size but how quickly the air changes simply because a few thousand trips that morning were made differently. It is a small, repeatable demonstration of something our cities have been slow to accept.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p class=&quot;p1&quot;&gt;Air quality is produced by ordinary design decisions about streets, buses, and land, not only by emergency measures taken when the season changes.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p class=&quot;p1&quot;&gt;Today is the seventh edition of the International Day of Clean Air for Blue Skies. This year&#039;s theme, Clean Air for Climate Action, is based on a new assessment by UNEP and the Climate and Clean Air Coalition that calculates the costs of 25 measures positively affecting both air quality and climate. It also provides a figure for the health and productivity benefits they generate. India has more reason than most to take this assessment seriously.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p class=&quot;p1&quot;&gt;According to the Global Burden of Disease study, output losses in India caused by air pollution amounted to $36.8 billion in 2019, about 1.36% of GDP. Delhi had the highest per-capita economic loss of all states and union territories, with Haryana second. The Clean Air Fund and the Confederation of Indian Industry estimate the annual cost to Indian business at around $95 billion, nearly 3% of GDP.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p class=&quot;p1&quot;&gt;Although clean air is currently presented only as a matter of pollution control, budget arguments favouring visible infrastructure continue to prevail. If it is described for what it really is—a matter of productivity, public health, and competitiveness—then it becomes an issue in which both finance and planning departments have a direct interest. This is the kind of framing we should adopt today.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p class=&quot;p1&quot;&gt;The good news is that India has many policies. The National Clean Air Programme includes 131 cities, each with a city-level action plan. GRAP provides the National Capital Region with a graded response. The FAME and state EV policies have advanced the shift towards electric vehicles.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p class=&quot;p1&quot;&gt;The Smart Cities Mission has equipped several cities with command centres and monitoring equipment. Nearly Rs 19,614 crore has been allocated under NCAP and air quality grants from the Fifteenth Finance Commission for 2019–20 to 2025–26, of which about Rs 11,211 crore has already been released. The challenge now is not to create a new scheme but to bring together what already exists so the various departments stop working at cross-purposes.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p class=&quot;p1&quot;&gt;Mobility is the largest lever a city government controls. Every kilometre shifted from a private vehicle to a bus, footpath, or cycle is a direct and measurable air quality gain. Unlike most interventions in this sector, it pays back in months rather than years.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p class=&quot;p1&quot;&gt;Raahgiri Day and the street redesign work underway across Haryana cities have run long enough to be treated as proof points. The Vision Zero and heat-resilient street design work in the state is good enough to be positioned as a replicable national model, letting India export its own template rather than import another&#039;s.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p class=&quot;p1&quot;&gt;The electric transition is the other half of this. Corporate Average Fuel Efficiency (CAFE-3) norms take effect from April 2027 and will push manufacturers towards cleaner fleets, with the small car exemption dropped from the revised draft and super credits offered for electric vehicles.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p class=&quot;p1&quot;&gt;PM E-DRIVE has set aside Rs 4,391 crore for 14,028 electric buses. PM e-Bus Sewa has sanctioned 10,000 buses for 116 cities at a total cost of Rs 57,613 crore, with another 35,000 planned under a follow-on scheme. However, the gap seems to be in delivery.&lt;/p&gt;
Only four cities, Guwahati, Bhavnagar, Nagpur, and Chandigarh, began operations in February 2026, and in some states the coordination committees needed to move the money have still not been constituted. Delhi notified its EV Policy in July 2026, targeting 30 per cent fleet electrification by March 2030, with electric-only three-wheeler registration from January 2027 and two-wheelers from April 2028.
&lt;p class=&quot;p1&quot;&gt;Haryana, Uttar Pradesh, and Rajasthan need policies of comparable ambition. A national EV target would ensure no city is left behind, because Bhubaneswar, Bengaluru, and Mumbai now face air quality problems, once treated as a northern concern.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p class=&quot;p1&quot;&gt;This is where the fault line appears. Most municipal authorities still treat continuous footpaths, safe cycling tracks, shade, and women-friendly street design as beautification efforts or, at most, road-safety entries. They should be considered part of clean-air infrastructure and included in the air-quality budget like any dust-suppression machine.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p class=&quot;p1&quot;&gt;As long as capital expenditure criteria favour concrete over tree canopy, we will continue funding infrastructure that reinforces car dependency. Gurugram alone is planning an elevated road costing Rs 800 crore, four flyovers between Vatika Chowk and Ghata, and about 135 kilometres of new service roads in newer sectors. Literature on induced demand shows the effect that additional road capacity has on vehicle kilometres travelled in the years after a road opens.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p class=&quot;p1&quot;&gt;The real problem is how fragmented things are. Each department concerned—transport, local urban bodies, environment, health, and revenue—is responsible for one part of the clean air issue, yet none is answerable for the final result. So no one owns the air. On the other hand, everyone owns part of the causes. Governance remains reactive and seasonal, with emergency actions only taken when the AQI rises above a certain level.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p class=&quot;p1&quot;&gt;Ongoing factors—vehicle dependence, construction dust, burning of waste and crop residue, and continuous loss of green and open space—receive far less attention than they should, given their contribution. Although data are available, they are not shared. The health department has information on respiratory disease burden, the transport department has data on vehicle registration growth, and the planning department has records on green cover loss.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p class=&quot;p1&quot;&gt;These datasets are rarely brought together when decisions are made. According to the Centre for Research on Energy and Clean Air&#039;s 2026 review, only 90 of 130 NCAP cities have carried out source apportionment studies. Many cities have been required to reduce particulate pollution by 40% without first establishing its causes.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p class=&quot;p1&quot;&gt;The same kind of rethinking should be applied to enforcement gaps. Dust regulations, emission checks, and bans on burning waste fail not mainly because departments lack the determination to enforce them, but because the wider urban system makes non-compliance the easiest option. These are design flaws, not compliance failures. Since we know how to correct design flaws, we can fix this.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p class=&quot;p1&quot;&gt;In December 2025, the World Bank approved $300 million for the Haryana Clean Air Project and $299.66 million for the Uttar Pradesh Clean Air Management Programme, part of its regional programme for the Indo-Gangetic Plains and Himalayan Foothills, covering 270 million people.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p class=&quot;p1&quot;&gt;These are India&#039;s first airshed-based, multi-sector state programmes. They address transport, industry, and agriculture together, funding electric buses and three-wheelers in Gurugram, Sonipat, and Faridabad, cleaner technology for MSMEs, and productive reuse of paddy stubble. Haryana has set up a special-purpose vehicle, ARJUN, to coordinate and monitor implementation across agencies.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p class=&quot;p1&quot;&gt;That is the kind of accountability structure our cities need, and it now exists in our own state. The airshed logic matters because nearly half of the PM2.5 affecting Indian cities is secondary particulate, formed from gases that travel across district and state boundaries. Industrial emissions, waste burning, and stubble burning will not be resolved within municipal limits.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p class=&quot;p1&quot;&gt;The decision facing each department is clear: we can continue managing the AQI figure, which gives a week&#039;s political relief every winter, or begin managing the systems that cause it, altering the baseline for good. We can have five departments carrying out separate, uncoordinated actions or establish a joint task force with a common accountability structure covering transport, urban development, environment, and health so the city takes a single, coherent course.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p class=&quot;p1&quot;&gt;We can bear the upfront costs of retrofitting streets for walking, cycling, and public transport or delay the cost and spread it over hospital beds, lost workdays, and long-term health system pressure. We can support proven, low-cost, community-rooted models that build demand for cleaner mobility street by street, rather than relying on a single capital-intensive demonstration project.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p class=&quot;p1&quot;&gt;NCAP&#039;s revised deadline for a 40 per cent cut in particulate pollution has passed, with a third of the money still unspent. The awareness these six clean air days aimed to build has largely been achieved. Winter will arrive on schedule regardless of what we say today.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p class=&quot;p1&quot;&gt;The only thing worth asking of officials marking the seventh is one shared action plan per city, delivered by December, that goes beyond paperwork and is built from a few clear components: each action must carry a specific timeline, designate the responsible department or officer by name, and be tied to a review mechanism that tracks progress at regular intervals with public reporting. Footpath and cycle track spending should be entered as air quality expenditure. Health, transport, and planning data should be reviewed together at least once before the air turns, ideally in joint sessions that set targets and check results.&lt;/p&gt;
A child cycling to school in any major metro lives with the consequences of that plan, or its absence, for roughly two hundred mornings a year, and she has no department to take it up with.
&lt;p class=&quot;p1&quot;&gt;&lt;i&gt;Sarika Panda Bhatt is an architect and urban planner and founder trustee of the Raahgiri Foundation. The views expressed are personal.&lt;/i&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
